<p><strong>The Parkins-Cooley Home</strong></p>
<p><strong>305 6th Ave NW</strong></p>
<p>This property was first listed in 1884 as the property of {% person "john-fogarty" %}John Fogarty{% endperson %} who owned and operated a railroad contractor company. The current home was constructed in 1910 by {% person "walter-parkin" %}Walter{% endperson %} and {% person "lucy-parkin" %}Lucy Parkins{% endperson %} and is a fine example of an Arts and Crafts style home. The interior still boasts the original pantry, woodwork and light fixtures.</p>
<p>Walter Parkins first came to Dakota Territory in 1884. He served as Indian Trader at {% place "fort-yates" %}Fort Yates{% endplace %} from 1889-1903 and was later appointed as a livestock purchasing agent on the {% place "standing-rock-reservation" %}Standing Rock Reservation{% endplace %}. He served as Vice President and sat on the Board of Directors of the {% place "merchants-national-bank" %}Merchants National Bank of Mandan{% endplace %} until his death in 1914. Upon his passing, his wife Lucy took in renters including a young lawyer named {% person "charles-cooley" %}Charles Cooley{% endperson %}, who met and fell in love with the Parkins’ daughter {% person "ruth-parkin-cooley" %}Ruth{% endperson %}. Charles Cooley and Ruth Parkins were married in 1919 and remained in the home with Ruth’s mother.</p>
<p class="source-credit">This photo taken in 1917 shows Bill, Mr. Parkin (Walter), in the middle, and his brother Henry on the left, as they were leaving for the ranch.</p>
<p>Mr. Cooley served as Mandan City Commissioner for eight years starting in 1928, and was instrumental in assuring a replacement building be constructed when the {% place "mandan-city-hall-before-1928" %}Mandan City Hall{% endplace %} burned down the night after his first city commission meeting. Today we refer to this replacement building as the {% place "collins-avenue-civic-building" %}downtown Fire Station{% endplace %}. It is depicted in a circa 1930 photo while is was still City Hall.</p>
<p>Mr. Cooley was also appointed to various civic advisory and state-wide boards, and served on the Board of Directors of the {% place "first-national-bank-building" %}First National Bank{% endplace %} for 22 years. The ownership of the home passed to the Cooleys in 1938 upon the death of Lucy Parkins. Charles Cooley remained in the home until his death in 1959. His widow Ruth remained in the home until 1981.</p>
